# SV-EXH-PROOF.3.3.4.b.1 (PGEN-SV-EXH-PROOF-0028, 2026-05-21): encode the
# LRM's `[ else statement_or_null ]` as OPTIONAL per IEEE 1800-2017 §A.6.6
# (PDF p.1164) and IEEE 1800-2023 §A.6.6 (PDF p.1201). The LRM is verbatim:
#   conditional_statement ::=
#       [ unique_priority ] if ( cond_predicate ) statement_or_null
#           { else if ( cond_predicate ) statement_or_null }
#           [ else statement_or_null ]
# Square brackets = OPTIONAL. The prior encoding (`&kw_else kw_else
# conditional_else_branch` with no `|` alternative for the no-else case)
# wrongly made the else mandatory, blocking every `if (1) $display("ok");`
# without an else. The `&X` PEG positive-lookahead is correct PEG syntax
# but here it was being used to MANDATE an LRM-OPTIONAL clause. The fix:
# wrap the else clause in `( … )?` using a helper rule that passes through
# the branch via `-> $2` (PEG-natural form; no `&X` / `!X` lookahead
# needed because the optional group is unambiguous — `?` is greedy and
# binds `else` to the innermost `if`, matching the SV dangling-else
# convention).
#
# The else-if chain `{ else if (…) statement_or_null }` is handled by the
# existing recursive `conditional_else_branch := conditional_statement |
# statement_or_null` — once the outer else is optional, the recursive
# `conditional_statement` arm naturally produces zero-or-more chained
# else-ifs.
#
# Historical: the previous EBNF contained a dead/mis-attached alternative
# at line 1116 (`| @sample: "if (a) ;" … !kw_else_ae050f5b`) that the
# original author reached for as the no-else form via PEG negative
# lookahead; due to the blank line and indentation it was parented to
# `conditional_else_branch` (unreachable from any actual parse path) and
# is removed here.
conditional_else_clause := kw_else_ae050f5b conditional_else_branch
                        -> $2

conditional_statement := @sample: "if (a) ;" ( unique_priority )? kw_if_958f57f5 lparen cond_predicate rparen statement_or_null ( conditional_else_clause )?
                      -> {unique_priority: $1, condition: $4, then_body: $6, else_body: $7}

# ⛔ `ordered` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT STYLE — do not "normalise" it to this file's
# other 29 `priority_first` uses (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.11a, PGEN-SV-CORPUS-GRAD-0197).
# The two arms are LANGUAGE-OVERLAPPING on `else if`: alt 2 reaches
# `conditional_statement` again via `statement_or_null → statement → statement_item
# → statement_item_sv_2017`, so on an `else if` BOTH arms parse the identical text
# and tie. Under a tournament policy every level therefore parses the whole
# remaining chain twice ⇒ T(k) = 2·T(k−1), measured as entries of exactly 2ⁿ−1 here
# and 12 362 MB on one 261-line opentitan crossbar. `priority_first` would NOT fix
# it — it changes only how a tie is RESOLVED, not whether the losing arm is
# explored (`grammar_wellformedness.rs`: under `longest_match` and `priority_first`
# the engine runs the FULL tournament). Only `ordered` short-circuits on the first
# winner, and alt 1 is the correct winner on a tie: `elseif` is the shape the LRM's
# `[ else statement_or_null ]` produces for a chained `else if`.
@branch_policy: ordered
conditional_else_branch := conditional_statement -> {kind: "elseif", body: $1}
                         | statement_or_null     -> {kind: "else",   body: $1}

