# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2s: the `!dot` guard is the SAME starving-star repair as `system_tf_call`,
# in the sequence family. IEEE 1800-2017 A.2.10 writes `sequence_list_of_arguments ::=
# [ sequence_actual_arg ] { , [ sequence_actual_arg ] } { , . identifier ( [ sequence_actual_arg ] ) }` —
# ordered arguments, THEN named ones. Spelled with a bare `( comma ( sequence_actual_arg )? )*` the
# greedy star ate the comma introducing the first NAMED argument, its inner optional matched EMPTY
# (a named argument starts with `.`, and no `sequence_actual_arg` can — measured: `.b(2)` and `.b` both
# REJECT as `sequence_actual_arg`, with `a+1` accepting as the control), and the named-argument star
# then needed a comma and saw `.`.
# ⛔ DEMONSTRATED AT SOURCE LEVEL: `sq(a ##1 b, .q(d))` goes REJECT -> ACCEPT, with the
# positional-only control `sq(a ##1 b, d)` parsing on both sides, so the flip is attributable to the
# MIX alone. ⭐ And the rule is genuinely reachable, which had to be checked before fixing it: on
# `sq(a ##1 b)` the tree carries this rule's own `ordered_tail`/`named_tail` fields, while on
# `sq(a, .q(b))` it carries the general `list_of_arguments` shape instead.
# ⛔ ABOVE the rule, never between its alternatives — see the note on `system_tf_call`.
sequence_list_of_arguments := sequence_named_arg ( comma sequence_named_arg )*
